> In chemistry, acid mixed with base creates a salt; eg
> HCl + NaOH = H2O + NaCL  (salt and water)

This is true, although you should use an arrow (->) not an equals (=) to
represent the direction of reaction.

> Thusly, if we mix a salt with an acid, a base will result.

I'm sorry? How did you get this result? HCl + NaCl doesn't react.

An acidic substance plus a neutral substance (as NaCl is) will *not* result
in a basic substance. If anything, the above equation can only result in:

(-HCl) + NaCL ~ NaOH + (-H2O)

iff we can agree on an acceptable definition of a "negative molecule".

> YANI possibility: from the reference on acids (and experience)
> Reaction of acid with pieces of metal can release
> flammable hydrogen gas.
> "You dip the long sword in a potion of acid -- more --"
> "the potion explodes!"

Unrealistic. The amount of hydrogen realeased is tiny, and it disperses into
the atmosphere as fast as it is released from the acid. Unless you managed
to get a particularly reactive metal - and I doubt there are chunks of
sodium lying around the gnomish mines...

One reaction I *can* think of which would fit is:

chalk + acid -> salt + water + CO2

(although in this case it'd be a calcium salt, but let's ignore that) - so
dipping a piece of chalk in acid would be another way to get salt water.
Chalk would also be good for scribbling on the ground (better than
dust-writing but worse than engraving), and curing slippery fingers (like
rock-climbers do).

